From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 May 2026 17:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] XLA cuda_executor: depend on //xla/tsl/cuda:nvml lazy stub

xla/stream_executor/cuda/cuda_executor.cc calls NVML directly
(nvmlDeviceGetHandleByPciBusId_v2, nvmlInit_v2, nvmlShutdown, etc.),
but the `cuda_executor` cc_library does not depend on
`//xla/tsl/cuda:nvml`. The `cuda_platform` sibling already does (see
the BUILD entry next to its rule), so any binary that links *both*
cuda_executor and cuda_platform resolves the NVML symbols through
TSL's in-tree lazy-dlopen stub (see xla/tsl/cuda/nvml_stub.cc, which
is aliased in under --@local_config_cuda//cuda:include_cuda_libs=false,
i.e. --config=cuda_wheel).

Mirror that dependency on cuda_executor so any binary that links
cuda_executor alone (host-side codegen / kernel-gen tools etc.) also
gets the stub. Without this fix, packagers that want a fully
hermetic-CUDA wheel layout (no DT_NEEDED libnvidia-ml.so.1) have to
inject -lnvidia-ml into LDFLAGS, which then leaks into every output
because Bazel reorders the --as-needed/--no-as-needed brackets.

Same trick `cuda_platform` already uses; resolves to `:nvml_stub`
under cuda_wheel and is a no-op otherwise.
---
--- a/third_party/xla/xla/stream_executor/cuda/BUILD
+++ b/third_party/xla/xla/stream_executor/cuda/BUILD
@@ -1338,6 +1338,7 @@ cc_library(
         "//xla/stream_executor/gpu:tma_metadata",
         "//xla/tsl/cuda",  # buildcleaner: keep
         "//xla/tsl/cuda:cudart",  # buildcleaner: keep
+        "//xla/tsl/cuda:nvml",  # buildcleaner: keep -- lazy-dlopen NVML stub
         "//xla/tsl/platform:env",
         "//xla/tsl/platform:errors",
         "//xla/tsl/platform:logging",
